## 11.3 Insurance — The Risk Transfer Toolkit

<div id="ol-ch11-2"></div>

Insurance is the primary mechanism by which the financial consequence of project risks is transferred from the developer to a third party — the insurer — in exchange for a premium. Insurance does not prevent losses; it converts the financial volatility of potential losses into the financial certainty of a premium expense, with the insurer absorbing the loss when it occurs (subject to policy limits, deductibles, exclusions, and conditions).

For a residential development project in Ontario, the insurance program operates as a portfolio of policies, each addressing specific categories of risk, with the portfolio designed so that the policies' coverages complement rather than overlap or leave gaps. The insurance broker — typically a specialized construction or real estate broker working with the developer — assembles the portfolio, negotiates terms with insurers, places coverage, and supports claim administration. The cost of broker services is typically embedded in the premium structure rather than billed separately.

The standard insurance policies on a residential high-rise development project in Ontario include the following:

### Builder's Risk (Course of Construction) Insurance

Builder's risk insurance — also called course of construction or COC insurance — covers physical damage to the project during construction. The policy responds to perils such as fire, lightning, windstorm, hail, vandalism, theft, water damage, and (depending on the policy form) flood, earthquake, and other named perils. Coverage is typically written on an "all risks" basis with named exclusions, providing broader protection than a "named perils" form.

The amount of coverage is typically the estimated completed value of the project, including hard costs and certain soft costs. Soft cost coverage — extension of the policy to cover items like additional interest expense, additional consultant fees, and re-mobilization costs that flow from a loss event — is a common and important extension that the Senior Analyst should verify is in place. Without soft cost coverage, a major loss event can leave the developer covering significant non-physical costs out of pocket while the physical rebuild is being funded by the insurer.

The policy period typically runs from the start of construction through substantial performance, with provisions for extension if substantial performance is delayed beyond the original projection. Builder's risk coverage typically transitions to property insurance at substantial performance, with careful coordination required to ensure no gap in coverage at the transition.

The deductible on builder's risk policies varies by peril, with water damage typically carrying a higher deductible than fire and named-storm or earthquake perils (where they apply) carrying significantly higher deductibles. The Senior Analyst should track the deductible structure as part of the project's contingency exposure — a $250,000 deductible on a water damage claim is a real budget exposure that contingency must be sized to absorb.

### Wrap-Up Liability Insurance

Wrap-up liability insurance — formally an Owner-Controlled Insurance Program or OCIP, sometimes structured as a Contractor-Controlled Insurance Program or CCIP — provides commercial general liability coverage for the developer, the construction manager, and all subcontractors and sub-subcontractors working on the project. The "wrap-up" structure consolidates liability coverage that would otherwise be carried separately by each contractor into a single policy purchased at the project level.

The advantages of the wrap-up structure include lower aggregate premium cost (volume purchasing power), elimination of coverage gaps between contractor policies, simplified claims administration, and the elimination of cross-litigation between contractors after a covered loss (since all are named insureds under the same policy). The disadvantages include the administrative complexity of enrolling each trade, the need to validate that each trade's regular liability coverage is appropriately suspended during the wrap-up period, and the potential for disputes about which losses are covered under the wrap-up versus elsewhere.

Wrap-up liability policy limits typically range from $10 million to $50 million for a residential high-rise project, with the limit determined by the project's complexity, the developer's risk appetite, and the lender's requirements. For projects above a certain size, additional excess or umbrella layers are added to provide higher aggregate limits.

The completed operations coverage period — the period during which the policy responds to liability claims arising from work performed during the construction period — is a critical consideration. For residential projects with extended statutory warranty periods (the seven-year Tarion structural warranty, for example), completed operations coverage of 10 years or more is increasingly standard. A policy that terminates completed operations coverage at substantial performance leaves the developer exposed for the entire warranty tail.

### Professional Liability (Errors and Omissions) Insurance

Professional liability insurance — also called errors and omissions or E&O insurance — covers the financial consequence of negligent professional services. For a development project, the relevant E&O policies are typically carried by the design consultants — the architect, the structural engineer, the mechanical and electrical engineers, the civil engineer, the envelope consultant, the landscape architect, and any other licensed professionals on the design team. Each consultant maintains its own E&O policy as a condition of its license and as a contractual requirement under its consulting agreement with the developer.

The developer's role in E&O coverage is verification rather than purchase. The Senior Analyst should confirm at project initiation that every consultant has E&O coverage of adequate limit (typically $2 million to $10 million per consultant depending on the consultant's role and the project's complexity), that the coverage period extends through the project's warranty exposure, and that the policy form is occurrence-based rather than claims-made (or, if claims-made, that adequate tail coverage is in place). Certificates of insurance are collected from each consultant at the time of contract execution and renewed annually.

A consultant whose E&O coverage lapses mid-project is a serious concern. If a design defect is discovered after the consultant's coverage has lapsed and the consultant is unwilling or unable to fund the rectification personally, the developer has no realistic recovery and absorbs the cost as an uninsured loss. Tracking consultant E&O coverage status is a defined Senior Analyst responsibility on most well-run projects.

### Tarion Security and Performance Guarantees

While Tarion enrollment is not insurance in the conventional sense, the security deposit posted with Tarion by the builder functions as a guarantee of the builder's warranty performance. The deposit is held by Tarion throughout the warranty period and is returned to the builder at the end of the period, less any draws made by Tarion to fund warranty work the builder failed to perform.

The deposit amount is determined by Tarion based on the builder's history, the number and value of units enrolled, and the builder's overall risk profile. For a large condominium project, the deposit can be substantial — millions of dollars — and ties up working capital for the duration of the warranty period.

### Title Insurance

Title insurance protects the developer (and, separately, the lender) against losses arising from defects in the project's land title — undisclosed liens, encroachments, errors in the survey, gaps in the chain of title, or other title defects that surface after acquisition. Title insurance is purchased once, at land acquisition, with a one-time premium that covers the entire period of the developer's ownership and (in some forms) extends to subsequent owners.

The Senior Analyst's involvement with title insurance is typically limited to verifying that coverage is in place at acquisition and that the policy is properly endorsed when the construction loan closes (the lender will require its own title coverage). Subsequent involvement arises only if a title defect surfaces, in which case the Senior Analyst tracks the claim and any recovery against the policy.

### Pollution Liability and Environmental Insurance

Pollution liability insurance covers losses arising from environmental contamination — either pre-existing contamination at the project site that surfaces during construction or contamination caused by construction activities. Standard general liability policies typically exclude pollution-related losses, which makes specialized environmental coverage essential for projects on sites with any environmental history.

For projects on brownfield sites — former industrial properties, gas stations, or other sites with documented environmental concerns — environmental insurance can take more elaborate forms, including coverage for cost overruns on the remediation program, coverage for re-opener of regulatory closure, and coverage for third-party claims arising from migration of contamination off-site.

### Directors and Officers (D&O) Liability Insurance

D&O insurance protects the individual directors and officers of the developer (and, depending on the structure, of the project entity) against claims of breach of fiduciary duty, mismanagement, misrepresentation, or other wrongful acts in their capacity as directors or officers. For a development project structured as a special-purpose entity with a defined board, D&O coverage is typically maintained at the entity level. For the developer's parent company, D&O coverage is maintained as a corporate policy that responds across all projects.

### Cyber Liability Insurance

A more recent addition to the standard insurance portfolio, cyber liability coverage responds to losses arising from data breaches, ransomware attacks, business email compromise, and other cybersecurity events. For a developer that holds purchaser personal and financial information, cyber coverage has become standard. The Senior Analyst's involvement is typically limited to ensuring that the developer's corporate cyber policy is in place and that the project's purchaser data handling is consistent with the policy's conditions.

### Business Interruption / Delay in Start-Up (DSU) Insurance

Delay in Start-Up insurance — also called soft cost or advance loss of profits insurance — covers the financial consequences of a delay in project completion caused by a covered builder's risk loss event. If a fire damages the project and pushes substantial performance back by six months, DSU coverage responds to the additional interest expense, additional consultant fees, lost rental income (for rental projects), and other financial losses associated with the delay. DSU is typically structured as an extension of the builder's risk policy and is often required by lenders on larger projects.

