## 11.4 The Insurance Procurement Process

<div id="ol-ch11-3"></div>

The development project's insurance program is typically placed several months before construction begins, with the timing coordinated against the construction start, the equity closing, and the construction loan closing. The procurement process involves multiple steps and produces a portfolio of policies that must align with the project's other documents and the lender's requirements.

### Step 1 — Selection of the Insurance Broker

The insurance broker is the developer's representative to the insurance market. Specialized real estate and construction brokers — typically operating within the construction practice of a national or international brokerage — bring market access, technical expertise, and claims advocacy that a generalist broker cannot match. For a residential high-rise project, the broker selection is one of the earliest pre-construction decisions, often made 12 to 18 months before construction start.

Broker selection criteria typically include: experience with comparable projects, depth of market access for the policies required, claims handling capability, technical depth in coverage analysis, and the strength of the broker's relationships with the carriers that lead the relevant markets.

### Step 2 — Project Underwriting Submission

The broker prepares a detailed underwriting submission describing the project — its location, size, construction type, schedule, contractors, design team, the developer's experience, and the developer's claims history. The underwriting submission is the document on which insurers will quote terms, so the quality of the submission directly affects the pricing and the breadth of coverage the developer can obtain.

The Senior Analyst typically provides input to the underwriting submission, including the project budget, schedule, and the construction manager's contract structure. Inaccurate or incomplete submission data can produce coverage gaps or claim disputes later.

### Step 3 — Market Solicitation and Quotation

The broker submits the underwriting package to the carriers most active in the relevant market. For builder's risk on a Toronto high-rise, the leading markets include domestic insurers, Lloyd's syndicates accessed through London-based intermediaries, and specialty markets accessed through Bermuda. The broker typically solicits quotes from three to six markets for each major policy and presents a comparison to the developer.

Quotation review focuses on premium cost, but the more important elements are coverage form, deductible structure, sublimits, exclusions, and conditions. A policy that is 15% cheaper but excludes water damage or imposes a $1 million deductible on the largest credible loss is not actually cheaper.

### Step 4 — Binder and Policy Issuance

Once terms are agreed, the broker arranges a binder — a written confirmation that the carrier has accepted the risk and will issue a policy on the agreed terms. The binder typically becomes effective on the agreed inception date, with the formal policy following 30 to 90 days later. The Senior Analyst should track that binders are converted to formal policies within the carrier's stated timeframe, and that the policy wording matches the binder. Discrepancies between binder and policy are not unusual and must be resolved before they become claim disputes.

### Step 5 — Certificate Distribution

Certificates of insurance — summary documents showing the policy's existence, limits, and named insureds — are distributed to the parties that require evidence of coverage. The construction lender will require certificates naming the lender as loss payee on the builder's risk policy and as additional insured on the wrap-up liability policy. The municipality may require certificates for street use and crane operation permits. Adjacent property owners may require certificates for shoring agreements or encroachment licenses.

Certificate management is a recurring administrative discipline throughout the construction period. Each policy renewal triggers a new round of certificates; each new party added to the project (a new tenant of an adjacent property, for example) may trigger a certificate request. The Senior Analyst typically maintains the certificate register or oversees its maintenance by the broker.

### Step 6 — Coverage Monitoring and Renewal

Insurance is not a one-time procurement event. Builder's risk and wrap-up liability policies typically have terms of one to three years, with renewal required as the project extends. Premium payment is typically scheduled annually or in installments; the Senior Analyst tracks premium payments against the budget and ensures premiums are paid on time to avoid coverage lapse. Mid-term changes to the project — a value increase from cost overruns, a schedule extension, an additional contractor — may require policy endorsements to maintain coverage adequacy.

