## 11.5 Bonds and Other Guarantees

<div id="ol-ch11-4"></div>

Bonds are a category of financial guarantee that, while not insurance in the strict sense, perform a related risk-transfer function. A bond is a three-party agreement among the principal (the party whose performance is guaranteed), the obligee (the party to whom the performance is owed), and the surety (the party that guarantees performance and pays in the event of principal default).

The most common bond types on a development project:

**Performance bonds** guarantee that the contractor will complete the work in accordance with the contract. If the contractor defaults, the surety either funds the cost of having another contractor complete the work or pays the obligee an amount up to the bond's penal sum. Performance bonds are typically written for 50% to 100% of the contract value.

**Labour and material payment bonds** guarantee that the contractor will pay its subcontractors and suppliers. If the contractor fails to pay, the unpaid subs and suppliers can claim against the bond. Payment bonds protect the developer from the risk of subcontractor liens against the project arising from the contractor's failure to pay downstream.

**Bid bonds** guarantee that a contractor that has submitted a bid will, if awarded the contract, execute the contract on the terms bid. Bid bonds are common on public-sector procurement but less common on private development.

**Maintenance bonds** guarantee that the contractor will perform warranty work during a defined post-completion period. Maintenance bonds are most common on infrastructure work performed within the municipal right-of-way, where the municipality requires the bond as a condition of accepting the work.

### Bonding Practices in the GTA Market

Bonding practices in the Greater Toronto Area private development market are notably different from public-sector practice. For municipal infrastructure work — sewer, watermain, road work within the municipal right-of-way — bonding is typically required by the municipality. For private vertical construction, however, bonding of trade contractors is less common in the GTA than in many other North American markets. Instead, construction managers and developers typically rely on prudent trade selection, financial review of trade contractors during prequalification, careful management of trade progress draws, and (on some projects) retainage above the statutory holdback as the primary mechanisms for managing trade default risk.

The reasoning behind this practice has several components: the cost of bonds on trade contracts is typically 1% to 3% of contract value, which on a large project aggregates to a meaningful expense; the bonding capacity of many smaller and mid-sized GTA trade contractors is limited; the construction manager's own performance bond (if required by the developer) provides some coverage of trade default risk indirectly; and the statutory holdback under the Construction Act provides a buffer of 10% on each progress payment that is available to address performance issues during construction.

The lender's requirements on bonding vary. Some construction lenders require performance and payment bonds from the construction manager; others accept a parent company guarantee, a letter of credit, or other forms of security in lieu of bonding. The Senior Analyst should confirm at loan closing what specific security is required and that the documentation is in place.

### Letters of Credit

Letters of credit are bank-issued instruments that guarantee payment up to a specified amount upon presentation of defined documents by the beneficiary. Letters of credit are common on development projects as alternatives to or supplements to bonds — used to secure development charge deferrals, to support performance obligations under development agreements with the municipality, to secure environmental remediation obligations, or to support contractor performance under construction contracts.

The cost of a letter of credit is typically lower than the cost of a bond (a typical LC carries a fee of 0.5% to 1.5% per year of the face amount) but the letter of credit ties up the developer's credit facility capacity, which has an opportunity cost. The Senior Analyst tracks outstanding letters of credit as part of the project's financial position and as part of the developer's overall credit utilization.

### Parent Company Guarantees

For projects developed through special-purpose entities — which is the typical structure for most residential high-rise projects — parent company guarantees are a common form of credit support. The parent company (the developer's operating entity) guarantees the performance of the project entity to the lender, to major contractors, or to the municipality. Parent guarantees are negotiated as part of the project's financing and contractual structuring and reflect the credit standing of the parent.

The Senior Analyst's involvement with parent guarantees is typically documentation-focused: ensuring guarantees are properly executed, that they remain in force throughout their intended period, and that any conditions or limitations are tracked. A parent guarantee that has expired or that has been undermined by a corporate reorganization is a material risk to the parties that relied on it.

