## 11.7 Lender Insurance Requirements and Monitoring

<div id="ol-ch11-6"></div>

The construction lender's interest in the project's insurance program is direct and contractual. The construction loan agreement will contain detailed provisions specifying the insurance the developer must maintain, the policy forms acceptable to the lender, the limits required, the deductible thresholds permitted, and the manner in which the lender is named on each policy.

### Loan Agreement Insurance Provisions

A typical construction loan agreement requires the borrower to maintain at minimum:

- Builder's risk insurance for not less than the full insurable value of the project, with the lender named as loss payee under a standard mortgage clause and with a deductible not exceeding a specified amount.
- Commercial general liability insurance (typically through a wrap-up structure) with limits of not less than a specified amount (often $10 million to $25 million), with the lender named as additional insured.
- Delay in start-up coverage for not less than a specified period (often 12 months) of the project's projected interest carry and operating cost.
- Such other coverages as the lender may reasonably require, including pollution liability, professional liability of the consultants, and others.

The lender's requirements are typically stated as minimums; the developer often carries higher limits than the lender requires, reflecting the developer's own risk appetite and the broker's recommendations.

### Loss Payee and Additional Insured Status

The lender's status on each policy matters in claim scenarios. As loss payee on the builder's risk policy, the lender has the right to receive insurance proceeds directly and to control their application (typically requiring proceeds to be applied to rebuild the damaged work). As additional insured on the wrap-up liability policy, the lender has independent rights as an insured against claims arising from the project, protecting the lender against, for example, a tort claim by an injured worker that names the lender as a defendant on the theory that the lender controlled the project.

The Senior Analyst should verify that the lender's loss payee and additional insured language on each certificate matches the loan agreement's requirements. Discrepancies in language are surprisingly common and can produce disputes when a claim arises.

### The Lender's Project Monitor

Most construction loans on Ontario high-rise projects are monitored by a third-party project monitor — a firm engaged by the lender (but typically paid by the developer through the loan budget) to conduct site reviews, review draw requests, and report monthly to the lender on project status. The monitor's review of insurance is a standard element of the monthly review: confirmation that policies are in force, that certificates reflect required terms, that premium payments have been made, and that no policy changes have occurred that would affect the lender's protection.

The Senior Analyst typically interacts with the monitor monthly during the draw cycle and should be prepared to provide insurance documentation on request. A pattern of incomplete or inaccurate insurance documentation in the draw package can produce delays in draw approval and, in extreme cases, can trigger lender concerns that escalate beyond the routine monthly cycle.

