## 11.8 Claims Management

<div id="ol-ch11-7"></div>

Insurance is most visible in the project's records when nothing has gone wrong — premium payments, certificate distribution, policy renewals. When something does go wrong and a claim is filed, insurance becomes a complex operational matter with significant Senior Analyst involvement.

### The Claim Process

When a loss event occurs — a fire, a water damage event, a third-party injury claim, a theft — the developer's first step is notification. Every insurance policy contains a notice provision specifying when and how the insurer must be notified of a potential claim. Late notice is one of the most common grounds for insurer denial of coverage; the discipline is to notify the broker immediately upon any potentially covered event, even if the developer is uncertain whether a claim will ultimately be made, and to let the broker manage notification to the carrier.

After notification, the insurer typically appoints an adjuster to investigate the claim. For property claims, the adjuster inspects the damage, reviews the policy, reviews the developer's claim documentation, and (after agreement with the developer or, in disputed cases, after appraisal or litigation) issues payment. For liability claims, the insurer typically assumes defense of the claim, with defense counsel selected by the insurer (subject to consent rights that vary by policy).

### Documentation Requirements

Claim documentation is typically extensive. For a property claim under the builder's risk policy, the developer is typically required to provide: detailed inventory of damaged work, photographs of the damage, scope and cost of repair or replacement, evidence of the loss being a covered peril, and evidence of the financial cost being incurred. For soft cost claims, additional documentation of the delay impact, the additional interest expense, the additional consultant fees, and other soft cost elements is required.

The Senior Analyst typically plays a central role in claim documentation, particularly for the financial elements. The cost report's segregation of claim-related costs (often through a defined cost code added for the loss event) allows the developer to demonstrate exactly what costs are being claimed. Documentation discipline at the time costs are incurred — rather than reconstructed after the fact — substantially improves recovery and reduces disputes with the insurer.

### Deductible Application

Every claim is subject to the applicable policy deductible. The deductible is the developer's retained portion of the loss — the first dollars of loss that the developer absorbs before the insurance pays. For a $500,000 water damage loss subject to a $50,000 deductible, the insurer pays $450,000 and the developer absorbs $50,000.

The deductible is typically charged to the project's contingency in the cost report. The Senior Analyst should ensure that the deductible is correctly coded and that the recovery from the insurer is correctly coded as an offset to the gross loss, producing a net cost equal to the deductible. Errors in this coding can produce a misleading picture of contingency utilization.

### Subrogation and Recovery

After paying a claim, the insurer may have subrogation rights — the right to step into the developer's shoes and pursue recovery from a third party that caused the loss. If the loss was caused by a contractor's negligence, the insurer may pursue the contractor (or, more commonly, the contractor's general liability insurer) for reimbursement. The waiver of subrogation provisions discussed in Section 11.6 above limit this in many circumstances on construction projects.

When subrogation produces a recovery, the developer may share in the recovery to the extent that the developer's loss exceeded the insurance payment (the deductible amount, plus any losses exceeding policy limits). The Senior Analyst tracks subrogation recoveries as additional offsets to the original loss.

### Reserve Adjustments

When a claim is filed and a reasonable estimate of the loss is available, the project's cost report should reflect the gross expected loss, the expected insurance recovery, and the net expected loss to the project. As the claim progresses and the actual loss and recovery are determined, the reserves are adjusted to reflect actual outcomes. The Senior Analyst's discipline is to update these reserves monthly, with the development manager's review, so that the project's financial position reflects realistic estimates rather than stale assumptions.

