## 11.9 Force Majeure and Extraordinary Events

<div id="ol-ch11-8"></div>

Force majeure — the legal doctrine and contractual provision that addresses events outside the reasonable control of the parties — has received renewed attention since the COVID-19 pandemic, which produced unprecedented disruption to development projects worldwide. The events of 2020 to 2022 demonstrated that force majeure provisions, drafted in calm times, must operate in turbulent times, and that the precise wording of those provisions materially affects who absorbs the financial consequence.

### Force Majeure in Construction Contracts

Most construction contracts contain a force majeure clause that, when triggered, excuses the contractor's failure to perform on schedule and may extend the schedule by the duration of the force majeure event. The classic triggering events include war, terrorism, civil unrest, severe weather beyond reasonable expectation, governmental orders prohibiting performance, and similar extraordinary circumstances. Pandemics, prior to 2020, were rarely specifically enumerated in force majeure clauses; the experience of the pandemic has led to widespread amendment of construction contract templates to include pandemic and epidemic events, government-ordered shutdowns related to public health, and supply chain disruptions arising from extraordinary circumstances.

The financial consequence of force majeure varies by contract. The typical CCDC contract provisions (the standard construction contracts in Canada published by the Canadian Construction Documents Committee) extend the time for performance without giving the contractor an automatic right to additional cost compensation. The contractor's general conditions and overhead during the extended period are absorbed by the contractor, not passed through to the developer, unless the contract specifically provides otherwise. Some negotiated contracts modify this allocation, particularly for events of extended duration.

### Insurance Response to Force Majeure

Standard insurance policies typically exclude certain force majeure events outright — war, civil unrest, and acts of terrorism are commonly excluded from property and liability coverage, with separate terrorism coverage available as a specific endorsement or standalone policy. Pandemic-related losses are typically not covered by builder's risk policies, where the policies require physical damage as a trigger and pandemic disruption typically does not produce direct physical damage to insured property. Business interruption coverage, similarly, typically requires a physical damage trigger.

The pandemic experience produced extensive litigation across the insurance industry about whether pandemic-related business interruption losses were covered under existing policies, with mixed outcomes that varied by policy wording, jurisdiction, and specific facts. The Senior Analyst's pragmatic stance, post-pandemic, is to assume that pandemic-related losses are not insured, that the project must absorb such losses through contingency or through the developer's balance sheet, and that mitigation depends on operational adaptation rather than insurance recovery.

### Cost and Schedule Impact of Extraordinary Events

When an extraordinary event occurs and produces project disruption, the Senior Analyst's role is to track the cost and schedule impact systematically. A new cost code for the event allows segregation of costs that flow from the event — additional labour, additional materials due to supply disruption, demobilization and re-mobilization costs, extended general conditions, additional insurance premiums, additional financing cost. The schedule impact is tracked through the construction schedule and the corresponding interest reserve impact in the loan budget.

For events that may produce insurance claims, contract claims against contractors, or other recoveries, the segregated tracking allows the developer to support those claims with detailed documentation. For events that produce uninsured loss, the segregated tracking allows the developer to understand the magnitude of the absorbed cost and to evaluate whether contingency is adequate or whether additional capital is required.

